 "standard_code": "N-RN.A.2",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/HSN/RN/A/2/",
 "standard_text": "Rewrite expressions involving radicals and rational exponents using the properties of exponents.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra 1 rational exponents worksheets (N-RN.A.1, N-RN.A.2): exponent rules with variables, zero and negative exponents, writing radicals as rational exponents, evaluating numbers like 8^(2/3) and simplifying. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "x^(m/n) is the nth root of x, raised to the power m.",
 "key_sub": "Multiply powers: add exponents. Power of a power: multiply exponents. Negative exponent: reciprocal.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>Product: <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> · <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>+<i>b</i></sup></li><li>Quotient: <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> ÷ <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>−<i>b</i></sup></li><li>Power: (<i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>)<sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>ab</i></sup>, and (<i>xy</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>n</i></sup><i>y</i><sup><i>n</i></sup></li><li>Zero and negative: <i>x</i><sup>0</sup> = 1 and <i>x</i><sup>−<i>n</i></sup> = 1/<i>x</i><sup><i>n</i></sup></li></ul><p>A <b>rational exponent</b> is a root: <i>x</i><sup>1/<i>n</i></sup> = <sup><i>n</i></sup>√<i>x</i> and <i>x</i><sup><i>m</i>/<i>n</i></sup> = (<sup><i>n</i></sup>√<i>x</i>)<sup><i>m</i></sup>. The bottom number is the root and the top number is the power. All the same rules apply.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify (3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup><i>y</i>)<sup>3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Cube every factor: 27<i>x</i><sup>6</sup><i>y</i><sup>3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 27<i>x</i><sup>6</sup><i>y</i><sup>3</sup></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Evaluate 8<sup>2/3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Cube root of 8 is 2.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup>2</sup> = 4.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify <i>x</i><sup>1/2</sup> · <i>x</i><sup>3/2</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>½ + 3/2 = 2.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup></p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying exponents when you multiply powers: <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> · <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> = <i>x</i><sup>5</sup>, not <i>x</i><sup>6</sup>.</li><li>Forgetting the coefficient: (2<i>x</i>)<sup>3</sup> = 8<i>x</i><sup>3</sup>, not 2<i>x</i><sup>3</sup>.</li><li>Reading a negative exponent as a negative number: 2<sup>−3</sup> = ⅛, not −8.</li><li>Swapping root and power: 8<sup>2/3</sup> is the cube root of 8 squared, not the square root of 8 cubed.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is a rational exponent?",
   "a": "An exponent that is a fraction. x^(1/n) is the nth root of x, and x^(m/n) is the nth root of x raised to the power m."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you evaluate 16^(3/4)?",
   "a": "Take the 4th root first (2), then raise it to the 3rd power: 2³ = 8."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does a negative exponent mean?",
   "a": "The reciprocal: x^(−n) = 1/xⁿ. It does not make the number negative."
  },
  {
   "q": "Do exponent rules work with fractional exponents?",
   "a": "Yes. Add exponents when multiplying, subtract when dividing and multiply for a power of a power, just like with whole numbers."
  }
 ],
